course: world
lesson: 78
title: Dinosaurs and life long ago
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Life on Earth has changed over a very long time: the first living things were tiny single cells in the sea, and plants, animals and finally people came much, much later.
explain: Dinosaurs were reptiles that lived for about 165 million years and died out about 66 million years ago, very likely after a huge rock from space hit the Earth and changed the climate.
explain: We know about them from fossils: when an animal was buried quickly in mud or sand, its bones were slowly replaced by minerals and turned into stone; footprints, eggs and even droppings can become fossils too.
explain: No person ever saw a living dinosaur — the first humans appeared more than 60 million years after the dinosaurs died out — but birds are living relatives of dinosaurs.
explain: By looking at a fossil skull, scientists can tell what an animal ate: Tyrannosaurus had long, sharp, curved teeth for meat, while Triceratops had a beak and rows of grinding teeth for tough plants.
fact: Dinosaurs died out about 66 million years ago.
fact: A huge rock from space hit the Earth about 66 million years ago; its dust and smoke blocked the sunlight all over the world and changed the climate.
fact: People never lived at the same time as dinosaurs.
fact: Birds are descended from dinosaurs.
fact: A fossil is the remains or trace of a living thing turned into stone.
fact: Footprints, eggs and droppings can become fossils as well as bones.
fact: Fossils usually form when a living thing is buried quickly in mud or sand.
fact: Scientists who study fossils are called palaeontologists.
fact: Sharp, curved teeth show that an animal ate meat.
fact: Flat, grinding teeth show that an animal ate plants.
fact: The first living things on Earth were tiny single cells in the sea.
fact: Woolly mammoths, hairy relatives of elephants, lived during the last Ice Age, long after the dinosaurs.
exercise: When did the dinosaurs die out? | About 66 million years ago | About 2,000 years ago | About 66 thousand years ago | About 6 billion years ago
exercise: What is a fossil? | Remains or traces of a living thing turned to stone | A living animal that was frozen in ice and can still be found today | A stone carved to look like a bone | A rock that fell from space
exercise: How do most fossils form? | The living thing is buried quickly in mud or sand | The animal lies out in the sun for many years until it hardens | The bones are dropped into a hot fire and baked hard | The animal slowly turns into stone while it is still alive
exercise: Did people live at the same time as dinosaurs? | No, people came over 60 million years later | Yes, they hunted the big ones with long spears | Yes, they kept some of the small ones as pets | Only in the coldest countries, near the North Pole
exercise: Which living animals are relatives of dinosaurs? | Birds | Cats | Fish | Frogs
exercise: What most likely caused the dinosaurs to die out? | A huge rock from space changed the climate | People hunted all of them | They all fell ill from eating too much grass | They grew too big to find food
exercise: What do we call a scientist who studies fossils? | A palaeontologist | An astronomer | A geographer | A chemist
exercise: What did a dinosaur with long, sharp, curved teeth eat? | Meat | Grass | Fruit | Seaweed
exercise: What kind of teeth did plant-eating dinosaurs have? | Flat teeth for grinding | Long curved fangs | No teeth at all, only claws | Sharp hooks for tearing
exercise: Which of these can become a fossil? | A footprint in mud | A breath of air | A sound in a cave | A shadow on a rock
exercise: What were the first living things on Earth? | Tiny single cells in the sea | Giant dinosaurs walking on land | Tall trees and bright flowers | Furry mammals living in caves
exercise: When did woolly mammoths live? | During the last Ice Age | At the same time as Tyrannosaurus | Before the first fish | Only a hundred years ago
exercise: Which group did dinosaurs belong to? | Reptiles | Mammals | Amphibians | Insects
exercise: How do scientists know what dinosaurs looked like? | By studying their fossils | From photos taken long ago | From people who saw them | From paintings in old caves
test: A film shows cave people running from a Tyrannosaurus. What is wrong with it? | Humans and dinosaurs never lived at the same time | Tyrannosaurus could not run | Cave people lived on the Earth long before the dinosaurs | Tyrannosaurus only ate plants
test: A fossil skull has wide, flat teeth worn smooth. What did the animal probably eat? | Tough plants | Small fish | Other dinosaurs | Insects caught in flight
test: Why are fossils much more common from animals that lived near lakes and seas? | They were buried quickly in mud and sand | Water turns bones into stone at once | Land animals had no bones | Fish lived longer than land animals
test: Which is true? | A sparrow is a living relative of the dinosaurs | A lizard is a baby dinosaur | Dinosaurs still live hidden away in deep jungles today | Mammoths were a kind of dinosaur
test: Scientists find a trail of big three-toed footprints in rock. What does it show? | A dinosaur once walked on soft mud there | A dinosaur's bones are buried just below | A bird made them last year | The rock was cut by people
test: What would a palaeontologist need to find out what a dinosaur ate? | Its teeth or fossil droppings | The colour and pattern of its skin | The sound it made when it roared | Its name written in an old book
test: Put these in order from oldest to newest. | Single cells, dinosaurs, mammoths, people | Dinosaurs, single cells, people, mammoths | People, mammoths, dinosaurs, single cells | Mammoths, dinosaurs, single cells, people
test: Why did the rock from space affect animals all over the world? | Dust and smoke blocked the sun and changed the climate | It hit every country at the same time | It was bigger than the whole Earth | It made all the seas boil away
test: An animal died on dry rocks and was eaten by others. Why is it unlikely to become a fossil? | It was not buried quickly | It was too small to keep | Fossils only form in caves | Its bones were already stone
test: big curved sharp teeth on this dino, what'd it eat | Meat | Leaves | Grass | Fruit
test: so what wiped out the dinosaurs | A huge rock from space hit the Earth about 66 million years ago | Cavemen hunted them all down long ago | The last ice age froze them all | Woolly mammoths ate all their food
test: Mia says cavemen used to ride dinosaurs. true? | No, people never lived at the same time as dinosaurs | Yes, early people tamed the small dinosaurs | Yes, but only the dinosaurs that ate plants | No, dinosaurs lived long after the first people
test: a video said woolly mammoths were a kind of dinosaur. right? | No, mammoths lived in the last Ice Age, long after the dinosaurs | Yes, mammoths were furry dinosaurs | Yes, they lived alongside T. rex | No, mammoths lived before the dinosaurs
exam: Ascertain the approximate period which has elapsed since the extinction of the dinosaurs. | About 66 million years | About 66 thousand years | About 6 million years | About 660 million years
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning human beings and dinosaurs. | Human beings never lived at the same time as dinosaurs | Human beings hunted dinosaurs for food | Human beings and dinosaurs lived together briefly | Human beings tamed certain small dinosaurs
exam: Identify, from the evidence hereinafter enumerated, that which demonstrates that an animal ate plants. | Flat, grinding teeth | Sharp, curved teeth | Long, hooked claws | Large, forward-facing eyes
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the period in which woolly mammoths lived? | During the last Ice Age, long after the dinosaurs | During the same period as the dinosaurs | Before the first living things in the sea | During the present century in Siberia
